# SecureLogX Python-Side Model Training and ONNX Export Approach

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the Python-side workflow for preparing data, training the SecureLogX PII/NPI named-entity recognition model, evaluating the model, and exporting production-ready artifacts for Java-based inference.

The Python side is responsible for:

* Preparing labeled PII/NPI data.
* Normalizing messy raw logs into a consistent JSONL format.
* Sampling high-value unlabeled examples for annotation.
* Supporting manual or semi-automatic annotation.
* Merging all labeled datasets.
* Splitting data into train, development, and test sets.
* Training a Hugging Face token-classification model.
* Evaluating model quality using precision, recall, and F1.
* Exporting the trained model to ONNX.
* Exporting tokenizer and label metadata required by the Java runtime.

The Java side should not depend on Python at runtime. Python is used only for data preparation, training, evaluation, and artifact export.

---

## 2. High-Level Pipeline

The expected SecureLogX Python-side flow is:

```text
Base PII/NPI data
    -> prepare HF-ready labeled dataset
    -> train baseline model
    -> normalize new unlabeled logs
    -> sample informative records
    -> annotate selected records
    -> merge old + new labeled data
    -> split into train/dev/test
    -> retrain final model
    -> evaluate final model
    -> export ONNX + tokenizer + labels + metadata
    -> integrate artifacts into Java SecureLogX runtime
```

A simplified sequence:

```text
clean / prepare
    -> train baseline
    -> normalize
    -> sample
    -> annotate
    -> merge
    -> split
    -> retrain
    -> evaluate
    -> export
```

