## Step 13: Export Tokenizer

SecureLogX Java runtime must use the same tokenizer behavior as training.

Required tokenizer artifacts usually include:

```text
onnx/tokenizer.json
onnx/tokenizer_config.json
onnx/special_tokens_map.json
```

The most important file for Java integration is:

```text
onnx/tokenizer.json
```

Suggested command:

```bash
cp output_trf/model-best/tokenizer.json onnx/tokenizer.json
cp output_trf/model-best/tokenizer_config.json onnx/tokenizer_config.json
cp output_trf/model-best/special_tokens_map.json onnx/special_tokens_map.json
```

Important rule:

The tokenizer used in Java must match the tokenizer used during training. Otherwise, ONNX predictions may not align correctly to the original text.

---

## Step 14: Export Labels

The Java runtime needs label metadata to interpret ONNX output indices.

Required output:

```text
onnx/labels.json
```

Recommended `labels.json` format:

```json
{
  "id2label": {
    "0": "O",
    "1": "B-EMAIL",
    "2": "I-EMAIL",
    "3": "B-SSN",
    "4": "I-SSN"
  },
  "label2id": {
    "O": 0,
    "B-EMAIL": 1,
    "I-EMAIL": 2,
    "B-SSN": 3,
    "I-SSN": 4
  }
}
```

Suggested extraction source:

```text
output_trf/model-best/config.json
```

The final Java runtime should not guess label order. Label order must come from the exported metadata.

